Task 1: Install and Configure Jenkins Master
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Java
Jenkins requires Java to run. Let's start by updating the system and installing OpenJDK.

# Update package repository
sudo apt update

# Install OpenJDK 11 (required for Jenkins)
sudo apt install -y openjdk-11-jdk

# Verify Java installation
java -version
Subtask 1.2: Install Jenkins Master
Now we'll install Jenkins using the official repository.

# Add Jenkins repository key
curl -fsSL https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee \
    /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null

# Add Jenkins repository
echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
    https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee \
    /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

# Update package list
sudo apt update

# Install Jenkins
sudo apt install -y jenkins

# Start Jenkins service
sudo systemctl start jenkins

# Enable Jenkins to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable jenkins

# Check Jenkins status
sudo systemctl status jenkins
Subtask 1.3: Configure Jenkins Master Initial Setup
# Get the initial admin password
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Note: Copy this password as you'll need it for the web interface setup.

Open a web browser and navigate to http://localhost:8080. Complete the initial setup:

Enter the initial admin password
Select Install suggested plugins
Create an admin user with these details:
Username: admin
Password: admin123
Full name: Jenkins Admin
Email: admin@localhost
Keep the default Jenkins URL: http://localhost:8080/
Task 2: Set Up Jenkins Agent Nodes
Subtask 2.1: Create Agent User and Directory Structure
We'll create a dedicated user for the Jenkins agent and set up the necessary directories.

# Create jenkins-agent user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash jenkins-agent

# Create agent working directory
sudo mkdir -p /home/jenkins-agent/agent-workspace

# Set proper ownership
sudo chown -R jenkins-agent:jenkins-agent /home/jenkins-agent/

# Create SSH directory for the agent user
sudo mkdir -p /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh
sudo chown jenkins-agent:jenkins-agent /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh
sudo chmod 700 /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh
Subtask 2.2: Generate SSH Keys for Agent Communication
# Switch to jenkins user
sudo su - jenkins

# Generate SSH key pair (press Enter for all prompts to use defaults)
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/jenkins_agent_key

# Copy public key to agent user's authorized_keys
sudo cp ~/.ssh/jenkins_agent_key.pub /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh/authorized_keys

# Set proper permissions
sudo chown jenkins-agent:jenkins-agent /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh/authorized_keys
sudo chmod 600 /home/jenkins-agent/.ssh/authorized_keys

# Exit from jenkins user
exit
Subtask 2.3: Download Jenkins Agent JAR File
# Create directory for agent JAR file
sudo mkdir -p /home/jenkins-agent/bin

# Download the agent JAR file from Jenkins master
wget http://localhost:8080/jnlpJars/agent.jar -O /home/jenkins-agent/bin/agent.jar

# Set proper ownership
sudo chown jenkins-agent:jenkins-agent /home/jenkins-agent/bin/agent.jar
Task 3: Configure Master-Agent Connection
Subtask 3.1: Add Agent Node in Jenkins Web Interface
Open Jenkins web interface at http://localhost:8080
Log in with admin credentials (admin/admin123)
Navigate to Manage Jenkins → Manage Nodes and Clouds
Click New Node
Configure the agent with these settings:
Node name: linux-agent-1
Type: Select Permanent Agent
Click Create
Subtask 3.2: Configure Agent Node Settings
Fill in the agent configuration form:

Description: Linux Agent Node 1
Number of executors: 2
Remote root directory: /home/jenkins-agent/agent-workspace
Labels: linux ubuntu agent-1
Usage: Use this node as much as possible
Launch method: Launch agents via SSH
Host: localhost
Credentials: Click Add → Jenkins
Subtask 3.3: Add SSH Credentials
In the credentials dialog:

Kind: SSH Username with private key
Scope: Global
ID: jenkins-agent-ssh
Description: SSH key for Jenkins agent
Username: jenkins-agent
Private Key: Select Enter directly
Copy the private key content:
# Display the private key (copy this output)
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/jenkins_agent_key
Paste the private key content into the text area
Click Add
Subtask 3.4: Complete Agent Configuration
Continue with the agent configuration:

Credentials: Select jenkins-agent (SSH key for Jenkins agent)
Host Key Verification Strategy: Non verifying Verification Strategy
Availability: Keep this agent online as much as possible
Click Save to create the agent.

Task 4: Verify Master-Agent Communication
Subtask 4.1: Check Agent Connection Status
In Jenkins web interface, go to Manage Jenkins → Manage Nodes and Clouds
You should see linux-agent-1 in the node list
Click on the agent name to view its status
The agent should show as Connected with a green indicator
If the agent shows as offline, click Launch agent to manually start it.

Subtask 4.2: Create Test Job for Agent Verification
Let's create a simple job to test the master-agent communication:

From Jenkins dashboard, click New Item
Enter job name: test-agent-connection
Select Freestyle project
Click OK
Configure the job:

Description: Test job to verify agent connectivity
Restrict where this project can be run: Check this option
Label Expression: linux
Build Steps: Click Add build step → Execute shell
Add this shell script:

#!/bin/bash
echo "=== Agent Connection Test ==="
echo "Job running on: $(hostname)"
echo "Current user: $(whoami)"
echo "Working directory: $(pwd)"
echo "Java version:"
java -version
echo "=== Test completed successfully ==="
Click Save.

Subtask 4.3: Execute Test Job
Click Build Now to run the job
Wait for the build to complete
Click on the build number (e.g., #1) in the Build History
Click Console Output to view the results
You should see output indicating the job ran on the agent node.

Subtask 4.4: Verify Agent Workspace
Check that the agent workspace was created and used:

# List contents of agent workspace
sudo ls -la /home/jenkins-agent/agent-workspace/

# Check for job workspace
sudo find /home/jenkins-agent/agent-workspace/ -name "*test-agent-connection*" -type d
